  "card": {
    "agent_id": "c10-momentum",
    "display_name": "Confirmed Sector Momentum",
    "discipline": "momentum",
    "purpose": "trading",
    "thesis": "Momentum exists because information diffuses and positions build slowly: institutional flows into a strengthening sector are staged over weeks (mandate reviews, index-fund rebalancing lags, analysts revising estimates in sequence rather than all at once), and short-horizon traders and dip-buyers who fade the first leg of a move get run over and forced to chase, extending it further. The people on the other side are the ones who sold or shorted the early leg on a mean-reversion prior and are still underwater when the second leg arrives. This is a diffusion-and-crowding story, not a single mispriced fact, which is why it decays slowly rather than being arbitraged away overnight. The entry requires three things at once: a short-term breakout (close crossing above its 20-day average), that breakout occurring above the 100-day average (a cheap, conventional context filter -- do not fight the intermediate trend), and RSI(14) above 55 confirming the move actually has thrust rather than being a one-bar wiggle. I measured this filter directly (see memo) and it is NOT a strong lever: it modestly cuts trade count in both chop windows (-8% in chop-2015, -4% in chop-2011) but its effect on average per-trade return is small and inconsistent, and in gfc it is measurably worse than the unfiltered rule on a per-trade basis. The real defense against chop in this design is the fast discretionary exit (price closing back below its own 20-day average), which keeps the average holding period to 6-13 sessions across every regime I measured, including both chop windows -- short enough that a whipsaw round-trip costs a small, bounded amount rather than compounding into a large loss. In both chop windows tested, zero trades reached the 8% stop within a 40-day hold; the stop is a tail backstop, not the routine risk control. I am not claiming this rule profits from chop -- it is roughly flat to modestly positive per trade in both chop windows, which is the honest finding: momentum does not get shredded here, but it does not have an edge here either, and the edge this rule has belongs to trending regimes.",
